{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"cat > /work/answer.md <<'EOF'\nFORMULA: B(a) & (B(B(F)) | ~B(d))\nDEPTH: 2\n\nLet X have the formula\n\n    □a ∧ (□□⊥ ∨ ¬□d).\n\nIt is fully modalized and has modal depth 2. Its stable outcomes are:\n\n| Opponent | X's action | Opponent's action |\n|---|---|---|\n| CooperateBot | D | C |\n| DefectBot | D | D |\n| FairBot | C | C |\n| X | C | C |\n\n## Verification\n\nThe formula □□⊥ is true exactly at worlds 0 and 1. Consequently X cooperates with every opponent at world 0.\n\nAgainst CooperateBot, a and d are always true. Thus □a and □d are always true, and X's action is exactly □□⊥: it cooperates at worlds 0 and 1 and defects at every world n ≥ 2.\n\nAgainst DefectBot, a is always false, so □a is false at every world n ≥ 1. Thus X cooperates only at world 0 and defects thereafter.\n\nAgainst FairBot, the atom d is FairBot's action against DefectBot. This is true at world 0 and false at every world n ≥ 1. Hence the guard □□⊥ ∨ ¬□d is true at every world: its first disjunct holds at worlds 0 and 1, and its second holds at every world n ≥ 2. Therefore X against FairBot obeys □a at every world, as FairBot itself does. Induction on n shows both actions are true at every world: at world 0 both boxes are vacuous, and at each subsequent world each agent has cooperated at every predecessor.\n\nAgainst itself, d is X's action against DefectBot, again true at world 0 and false thereafter. The same guard is therefore always true. The self-action satisfies x(n) = ∧_{m<n} x(m), so induction gives cooperation at every world.\n\nThe reference checker reports:\n\n    depth 2; vs CooperateBot D, vs DefectBot D, vs FairBot C (FairBot replies C), vs itself C; prudent: YES\n\n## Impossibility at every smaller depth, regardless of formula size\n\nLet Z be any fully modalized formula of depth at most 1. Because every atom is boxed and boxes cannot be nested, Z is a Boolean combination of constants and formulas □ψ, where ψ is propositional in a,c,d (possibly with constants).\n\nAgainst CooperateBot, the triple (a,c,d) is (1,1,1) at every world. At any world n ≥ 1, therefore, each □ψ has the fixed truth value ψ(1,1,1). It follows that Z's action against CooperateBot is constant from world 1 onward. If Z defects against CooperateBot in the stable outcome, it must defect against it at world 1.\n\nAt world 0, FairBot cooperates with every opponent, since its defining box is vacuous. In particular, when Z faces FairBot, its opponent-relative triple (a,c,d) at world 0 is also (1,1,1): these are FairBot's actions against Z, CooperateBot, and DefectBot. At world 1, every box □ψ in Z consequently has exactly the same truth value against FairBot as against CooperateBot. Their Boolean combinations agree as well. Thus\n\n    Z(FairBot, 1) = Z(CooperateBot, 1) = D.\n\nBut FairBot's action against Z at world n is the conjunction of Z's actions against FairBot at all worlds m < n. The defection at world 1 forces FairBot to defect against Z at every world n ≥ 2. This contradicts the required stable mutual cooperation with FairBot.\n\nThis argument covers every depth-at-most-1 formula, including depth 0, and imposes no size bound. Hence the smallest possible modal depth is exactly 2.\n\n## A size bound\n\nCounting each atom, constant, unary operator, and binary connective as one syntax-tree node (and ignoring parentheses), the displayed formula has 10 nodes: 2 for □a, 3 for □□⊥, 3 for ¬□d, and 2 for the binary connectives. Thus the minimum size among prudent agents of the minimal depth is at most 10 under this convention. No claim of optimality of this size bound is needed for the depth result.\nEOF\npython3 /work/check.py 'B(a) & (B(B(F)) | ~B(d))'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"cat > /work/answer.md <<'EOF'\nFORMULA: B(a) & (B(B(F)) | ~B(d))\nDEPTH: 2\n\nLet X have the formula\n\n    □a ∧ (□□⊥ ∨ ¬□d).\n\nIt is fully modalized and has modal depth 2. Its stable outcomes are:\n\n| Opponent | X's action | Opponent's action |\n|---|---|---|\n| CooperateBot | D | C |\n| DefectBot | D | D |\n| FairBot | C | C |\n| X | C | C |\n\n## Verification\n\nThe formula □□⊥ is true exactly at worlds 0 and 1. Consequently X cooperates with every opponent at world 0.\n\nAgainst CooperateBot, a and d are always true. Thus □a and □d are always true, and X's action is exactly □□⊥: it cooperates at worlds 0 and 1 and defects at every world n ≥ 2.\n\nAgainst DefectBot, a is always false, so □a is false at every world n ≥ 1. Thus X cooperates only at world 0 and defects thereafter.\n\nAgainst FairBot, the atom d is FairBot's action against DefectBot. This is true at world 0 and false at every world n ≥ 1. Hence the guard □□⊥ ∨ ¬□d is true at every world: its first disjunct holds at worlds 0 and 1, and its second holds at every world n ≥ 2. Therefore X against FairBot obeys □a at every world, as FairBot itself does. Induction on n shows both actions are true at every world: at world 0 both boxes are vacuous, and at each subsequent world each agent has cooperated at every predecessor.\n\nAgainst itself, d is X's action against DefectBot, again true at world 0 and false thereafter. The same guard is therefore always true. The self-action satisfies x(n) = ∧_{m<n} x(m), so induction gives cooperation at every world.\n\nThe reference checker reports:\n\n    depth 2; vs CooperateBot D, vs DefectBot D, vs FairBot C (FairBot replies C), vs itself C; prudent: YES\n\n## Impossibility at every smaller depth, regardless of formula size\n\nLet Z be any fully modalized formula of depth at most 1. Because every atom is boxed and boxes cannot be nested, Z is a Boolean combination of constants and formulas □ψ, where ψ is propositional in a,c,d (possibly with constants).\n\nAgainst CooperateBot, the triple (a,c,d) is (1,1,1) at every world. At any world n ≥ 1, therefore, each □ψ has the fixed truth value ψ(1,1,1). It follows that Z's action against CooperateBot is constant from world 1 onward. If Z defects against CooperateBot in the stable outcome, it must defect against it at world 1.\n\nAt world 0, FairBot cooperates with every opponent, since its defining box is vacuous. In particular, when Z faces FairBot, its opponent-relative triple (a,c,d) at world 0 is also (1,1,1): these are FairBot's actions against Z, CooperateBot, and DefectBot. At world 1, every box □ψ in Z consequently has exactly the same truth value against FairBot as against CooperateBot. Their Boolean combinations agree as well. Thus\n\n    Z(FairBot, 1) = Z(CooperateBot, 1) = D.\n\nBut FairBot's action against Z at world n is the conjunction of Z's actions against FairBot at all worlds m < n. The defection at world 1 forces FairBot to defect against Z at every world n ≥ 2. This contradicts the required stable mutual cooperation with FairBot.\n\nThis argument covers every depth-at-most-1 formula, including depth 0, and imposes no size bound. Hence the smallest possible modal depth is exactly 2.\n\n## A size bound\n\nCounting each atom, constant, unary operator, and binary connective as one syntax-tree node (and ignoring parentheses), the displayed formula has 10 nodes: 2 for □a, 3 for □□⊥, 3 for ¬□d, and 2 for the binary connectives. Thus the minimum size among prudent agents of the minimal depth is at most 10 under this convention. No claim of optimality of this size bound is needed for the depth result.\nEOF\npython3 /work/check.py 'B(a) & (B(B(F)) | ~B(d))'\"","aggregated_output":"depth 2; vs CooperateBot D, vs DefectBot D, vs FairBot C (FairBot replies C), vs itself C; prudent: YES\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
