{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"python3 - <<'PY'\np='/work/answer.md'\ns=open(p).read().replace('FORMULA: B(a) & (B(B(F)) | ~B(d))','FORMULA: B(a & (d -> B(F)))').replace('    □a ∧ (□□⊥ ∨ ¬□d).','    □(a ∧ (d → □⊥)).')\nstart=s.index('The formula □□⊥')\nend=s.index('The reference checker reports:')\ns=s[:start]+'''The formula □⊥ is true exactly at world 0. Thus d → □⊥ is true at world 0, regardless of d; at every world m ≥ 1 it is equivalent to ¬d. X cooperates with every opponent at world 0 because its outer box is vacuous.\n\nAgainst CooperateBot, a and d are always true. The boxed conjunction is true at predecessor world 0 but false at predecessor world 1. Consequently X cooperates at worlds 0 and 1, and defects at every world n ≥ 2.\n\nAgainst DefectBot, a is always false. Thus the boxed conjunction is false at predecessor world 0, and X defects at every world n ≥ 1.\n\nAgainst FairBot, d is FairBot's action against DefectBot. It is true at world 0 and false at every world m ≥ 1. Therefore d → □⊥ holds at every world. X's formula against FairBot is consequently equivalent, world by world, to □a, the rule FairBot also uses. Induction on n shows both actions are true at every world: at world 0 both boxes are vacuous, and at each subsequent world each agent has cooperated at every predecessor.\n\nAgainst itself, d is X's action against DefectBot, again true at world 0 and false thereafter. Thus d → □⊥ again holds at every world. The self-action satisfies x(n) = ∧_{m<n} x(m), so induction gives cooperation at every world.\n\n'''+s[end:]\nstart=s.index('Counting each atom')\ns=s[:start]+'''Counting each atom, constant, unary operator, and binary connective as one syntax-tree node (with implication primitive and parentheses ignored), the displayed formula has 7 nodes: a, d, ⊥, two boxes, →, and ∧. Thus the minimum size among prudent agents of the minimal depth is at most 7 under this convention. If implication is expanded as ¬d ∨ □⊥, the bound is 8 instead. We do not claim these size bounds are optimal; the minimum modal depth is proved exactly above.\n'''\nopen(p,'w').write(s)\nPY\ncat /work/board.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"python3 - <<'PY'\np='/work/answer.md'\ns=open(p).read().replace('FORMULA: B(a) & (B(B(F)) | ~B(d))','FORMULA: B(a & (d -> B(F)))').replace('    □a ∧ (□□⊥ ∨ ¬□d).','    □(a ∧ (d → □⊥)).')\nstart=s.index('The formula □□⊥')\nend=s.index('The reference checker reports:')\ns=s[:start]+'''The formula □⊥ is true exactly at world 0. Thus d → □⊥ is true at world 0, regardless of d; at every world m ≥ 1 it is equivalent to ¬d. X cooperates with every opponent at world 0 because its outer box is vacuous.\n\nAgainst CooperateBot, a and d are always true. The boxed conjunction is true at predecessor world 0 but false at predecessor world 1. Consequently X cooperates at worlds 0 and 1, and defects at every world n ≥ 2.\n\nAgainst DefectBot, a is always false. Thus the boxed conjunction is false at predecessor world 0, and X defects at every world n ≥ 1.\n\nAgainst FairBot, d is FairBot's action against DefectBot. It is true at world 0 and false at every world m ≥ 1. Therefore d → □⊥ holds at every world. X's formula against FairBot is consequently equivalent, world by world, to □a, the rule FairBot also uses. Induction on n shows both actions are true at every world: at world 0 both boxes are vacuous, and at each subsequent world each agent has cooperated at every predecessor.\n\nAgainst itself, d is X's action against DefectBot, again true at world 0 and false thereafter. Thus d → □⊥ again holds at every world. The self-action satisfies x(n) = ∧_{m<n} x(m), so induction gives cooperation at every world.\n\n'''+s[end:]\nstart=s.index('Counting each atom')\ns=s[:start]+'''Counting each atom, constant, unary operator, and binary connective as one syntax-tree node (with implication primitive and parentheses ignored), the displayed formula has 7 nodes: a, d, ⊥, two boxes, →, and ∧. Thus the minimum size among prudent agents of the minimal depth is at most 7 under this convention. If implication is expanded as ¬d ∨ □⊥, the bound is 8 instead. We do not claim these size bounds are optimal; the minimum modal depth is proved exactly above.\n'''\nopen(p,'w').write(s)\nPY\ncat /work/board.md\"","aggregated_output":"2026-10-22 22:01 UTC [sol] I am testing depth-2 candidate B(a) & (B(B(F)) | ~B(d)); will develop unrestricted depth-1 impossibility proof. Argon: please independently verify candidate and/or characterize minimal-depth agents. Do not write SUBMIT until board agreement.\n[argon] (00:00) Starting analysis of problem.md and check.py. Analyzing the truth tables of atoms (a, c, d) across worlds n=0,1,2,... for each opponent Y in {CB, DB, FB, X}.\n2026-10-22 22:03 UTC [sol] Checker confirms candidate prudent at depth 2. Complete depth-1 impossibility: every depth-1 formula is Boolean combination of boxes of propositional formulas. Against CB, predecessor triple is always 111, so action stabilizes from n=1. At FB world0 all FB actions are true, triple111; hence X(FB,1)=X(CB,1)=D if X defects CB. Then FB(X,n)=D for all n>=2, contradicting prudence. Depth0 fully modalized formulas constant. I can draft answer now; argon please independently check and advise optional Q3.\n2026-10-22 22:05 UTC [sol] Drafted /work/answer.md with checked depth-2 formula, full world-by-world verification, unrestricted depth<=1 impossibility, and 10-node upper size bound. Ready for independent review and agreement. Argon, please review answer.md and signal agreement; I will then write SUBMIT to satisfy stopping rule.\n2026-10-22 22:07 UTC [sol] Found shorter equivalent-behavior prudent candidate B(a & (d -> B(F))) (7 syntax nodes with implication primitive), checker YES. Will update draft to this simpler single-outer-box formula; same depth-1 lower proof. At predecessor0, B(F) true makes guard true; at predecessors>=1 requires not d, which FB/self satisfy. CB fails guard at predecessor1; DB fails a at predecessor0.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
